= Research on Temperature Effects

== Abstract

This study examines the effect of temperature on reaction rates in aqueous solutions.
The experiment involved fifty participants across three experimental conditions.

== Introduction

Chemistry is the science of matter and its transformations at the molecular level.
Reaction kinetics describes how quickly chemical reactions proceed over time.
Temperature is known to accelerate reactions following the Arrhenius equation.

== Methods

Samples were prepared at three temperatures: 20, 40, and 60 degrees Celsius.
Each trial lasted thirty minutes with constant stirring.
Measurements were recorded every five seconds throughout the trial.

== Results

The average reaction rate at 20°C was 0.05 mol per litre per second.
At 40°C the rate increased substantially to 0.15 mol per litre per second.
The highest temperature of 60°C yielded a rate of 0.45 mol per litre per second.

== Conclusion

Temperature has a strong positive effect on reaction rates in this system.
Future work should investigate the effect of pressure above atmospheric conditions.
